(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-mi-acs-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Fibrinolytics Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug group contraindicates nitrates because of the risk of severe hypotension?","choices":["Beta blockers","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors","Heparins","Fibrinolytics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors are a listed contraindication to nitrates, because together they can cause severe hypotension. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which pair of conditions makes up non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Unstable angina and non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Stable angina and non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Unstable angina and ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Variant angina and ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no ST elevation on the electrocardiogram the diagnosis is unstable angina or non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction, separated by a biochemical marker. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the shared mechanism of all fibrinolytics?","choices":["Inhibiting thromboxane A2 synthesis","Neutralizing activated factor X","Converting plasminogen to plasmin","Blocking adenosine diphosphate receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All fibrinolytics act directly or indirectly to convert plasminogen to plasmin, which lyses the fibrin clot. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which immune reaction is listed for all fibrinolytic agents?","choices":["Allergic reactions only with streptokinase","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Delayed contact dermatitis","Allergic reactions including anaphylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Allergic reactions, including anaphylactic reactions, are listed for all fibrinolytic agents; fever, chills and rash occur mainly with streptokinase. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which blood pressure finding contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Severe uncontrolled hypertension","Well-controlled hypertension","Mildly raised pressure from pain","Low-normal blood pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe uncontrolled hypertension contraindicates fibrinolytics because of the risk of intracranial bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"With no ST elevation on the electrocardiogram, what separates unstable angina from non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["The duration of the chest pain","The response to nitroglycerin","The presence of Q waves on the tracing","A biochemical marker in the blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A biochemical marker of myocardial injury is positive in infarction and negative in unstable angina, which is how the two non-ST-elevation diagnoses are separated. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Where do the assembled clotting factor complexes continue the cascade during propagation?","choices":["On the surface of intact endothelium","Inside the fibrous cap of the plaque","Along smooth muscle cell membranes","On the surface of activated platelets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Assembled complexes on activated platelets convert prothrombin to thrombin and fibrinogen to fibrin and stabilize the clot. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Why are fibrinolytics not recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["They cannot be combined with aspirin","They cannot be combined with heparins","Bleeding risk outweighs benefit","They cause coronary vasospasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome the bleeding risk is greater than the benefit, so fibrinolytics are not recommended. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How do alteplase, reteplase and tenecteplase compare in their responses and adverse effects?","choices":["They are very similar","Reteplase causes far less bleeding","Tenecteplase carries no bleeding risk","Alteplase alone causes allergic reactions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The three recombinant tissue plasminogen activators produce very similar responses and adverse effects, chiefly bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When should a patient with suspected acute coronary syndrome take aspirin?","choices":["Only after arriving at the hospital","After the pain has lasted an hour","At the first signs of chest pain","Once the electrocardiogram confirms infarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is used at the first signs of chest pain; early use reduces mortality and reinfarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which history is a contraindication to aspirin in suspected acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Recent intracranial hemorrhage","Prior myocardial infarction","Previous stent placement","Hyperlipidemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is contraindicated with allergy, a recent gastrointestinal bleed or a recent intracranial hemorrhage because it raises bleeding risk. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is a fibrinolytic?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Tenecteplase","Clopidogrel","Metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tenecteplase is a recombinant fibrinolytic, like alteplase and reteplase. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which recent event contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["A recent blood draw","A recent dental cleaning","A recent vaccination","Recent major surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recent surgery (including organ biopsy, puncture of noncompressible vessels, serious head or facial trauma and cardiopulmonary resuscitation) contraindicates fibrinolytics because of bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which neurologic history contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Migraine headaches","Childhood febrile seizure","Active intracranial tumor","Essential tremor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An active intracranial process such as a tumor contraindicates fibrinolytics because of the risk of intracranial hemorrhage. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which benefit do beta blockers provide after acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Dissolution of the coronary clot","Smaller infarct size, fewer sudden deaths","Chest pain relief with no outcome benefit","Blockade of platelet aggregation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers reduce infarct size, heart failure incidence and sudden cardiac death. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which mediator links tissue injury, coagulation and the platelet response?","choices":["Thrombin","Plasmin","Fibrinogen","Tissue plasminogen activator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin and also elicits multiple platelet responses, tying injury, coagulation and platelet activation together. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which finding contraindicates nitrates in suspected acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Hypertension","Hypotension","Ongoing chest pain","Elevated cardiac biomarkers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension is a listed contraindication, because nitrates lower blood pressure further. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How does thrombin amplify its own production?","choices":["By activating plasminogen to plasmin","By activating factors V and VIII","By blocking the action of tissue factor","By converting fibrin back to fibrinogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During amplification thrombin activates platelets and factors V and VIII, which assemble with factors IXa on activated platelets to make more thrombin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How are nitrates given as acute coronary syndrome care progresses?","choices":["Intravenous infusion, then sublingual","Sublingual, then intravenous infusion","Oral, then transdermal patch","Sublingual, then oral extended-release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Therapy starts sublingually until the patient is in the hospital, then changes to an intravenous infusion. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which bleeding history contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Occasional spotting from hemorrhoids","A nosebleed during childhood","Recent serious gastrointestinal bleeding","Bruising after a minor fall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serious gastrointestinal bleeding in the recent past is a listed contraindication, since fibrinolytics could restart serious bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which drug manages chest pain that does not respond to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Metoprolol","Aspirin","Morphine","Alteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Morphine, an opioid analgesic, is used for chest pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which drug class is used before percutaneous coronary intervention and to prevent clots after stent placement?","choices":["Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors","Fibrinolytics","Beta blockers","P2Y12 receptor antagonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. P2Y12 receptor antagonists, which block the adenosine diphosphate receptor on platelets, are used before percutaneous coronary intervention and as clot prophylaxis with stent placement. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which finding calls for caution before giving a beta blocker in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Premature ventricular beats","Hypertension","Heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Heart block is a listed caution, along with bradycardia, hypotension and severe reactive airway disease, because beta blockers slow conduction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What should be watched for closely after fibrinolytic therapy?","choices":["Signs of bleeding","Blood glucose falls","Potassium elevation","Reduced urine output"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding, including intracranial hemorrhage, is the main risk of fibrinolytics, so the patient is monitored for it. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
